// TASK 2: Encryption Fundamentals
$task_contents[2] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Building a Shared Vocabulary</h2>

    <p>Encryption transforms readable data, called plaintext, into an unreadable form called <strong>ciphertext</strong>. Only someone holding the correct key can reverse that transformation. Every control covered in this room, whether it protects data moving across a network or sitting on a disk, is built on this same basic idea.</p>

    <p>There are two broad families of encryption. Symmetric encryption uses one key for both encrypting and decrypting, which makes it fast but requires that key to be shared safely between parties. Asymmetric encryption uses a public and private key pair, which solves the sharing problem but is slower, so it is often used to exchange a symmetric key rather than to encrypt bulk data directly.</p>

    <p>This room organizes everything around a simple model: data exists in one of three states at any given moment. It is either moving between systems (in transit), stored somewhere (at rest), or actively being processed (in use). Each state has its own risks and its own set of controls.</p>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Scope of Encryption</h4>
        <p>Encryption protects confidentiality. It does not, by itself, guarantee integrity or availability. A file can be encrypted and still be deleted, corrupted, or replaced with a different encrypted file. Other controls handle those risks.</p>
    </div>

