// TASK 3: Encryption in Transit
$task_contents[3] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Protecting Data on the Move</h2>

    <p>Data in transit is exposed the moment it leaves one system and travels toward another. Anyone positioned on the path between the two endpoints could potentially read or alter it if it is not protected. This is the state most people picture first when they think about encryption.</p>

    <p><strong>TLS</strong> is the protocol behind the padlock icon in a browser. Before any data is exchanged, the client and server perform a handshake: the server presents a certificate proving its identity, the two sides agree on a shared key, and only then does the encrypted session begin. Think of TLS as an armored car booked for a single delivery between two known parties.</p>

    <p><strong>IPSec</strong> works differently. Instead of protecting a single connection, it creates an encrypted tunnel at the network layer between two locations, commonly used for site to site VPNs connecting offices or data centers. It is closer to building a private, permanent tunnel between two buildings rather than booking a single trip.</p>

    <p>Application-layer encryption is a third option, where the application itself encrypts specific fields or messages before they are sent, independent of whatever transport protocol carries them.</p>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> A Common Real-World Gap</h4>
        <p>A TLS connection can still be insecure if certificate validation is skipped or misconfigured. An attacker presenting a fake certificate can intercept traffic even though the connection appears encrypted. Validating the certificate chain is just as important as encrypting the traffic.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Comparing Transit Controls</h3>
    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
