// TASK 4: Encryption at Rest
$task_contents[4] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Protecting Stored Data</h2>

    <p>Data at rest is data sitting on a disk, in a database, or in a backup archive. It is not moving, but it is not safe just because it is sitting still. A stolen laptop, a misplaced backup tape, or a compromised storage system all expose data at rest to someone who never had to intercept a network connection.</p>

    <p><strong>Full-disk encryption</strong> protects an entire volume. Anyone who removes the drive or copies the raw disk image cannot read anything without the decryption key. It is the broadest and simplest control, similar to locking an entire warehouse.</p>

    <p><strong>TDE</strong>, transparent data encryption, works one level deeper. It encrypts the database files themselves, applied automatically by the database engine so that individual applications querying the database do not need to change any code.</p>

    <p><strong>Field-level encryption</strong> goes deeper still, encrypting only specific columns, such as a card number field, independently of everything else in the same table. This is like keeping the most valuable items in the warehouse inside their own locked safe, even though the warehouse itself is already locked.</p>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> What Encryption at Rest Does Not Cover</h4>
        <p>Encryption at rest protects data if the storage itself is stolen or accessed directly. It does not protect against a compromised application with legitimate database access, since the application decrypts the data as part of normal operation. Access controls still matter.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Comparing At-Rest Controls</h3>
    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Control</th>
                <th>Granularity</th>
                <th>Performance Impact</th>
                <th>Typical Use</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Full-disk encryption</td>
                <td>Entire volume</td>
                <td>Minimal</td>
                <td>Laptops, servers, backup media</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>TDE</td>
                <td>Entire database</td>
                <td>Low</td>
                <td>Databases holding sensitive records</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Field-level encryption</td>
                <td>Individual columns</td>
                <td>Higher, applied per field</td>
                <td>Highly sensitive fields like card numbers</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

