// TASK 5: Encryption in Use
$task_contents[5] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Protecting Data While It Is Being Processed</h2>

    <p>Data in use is the hardest state to protect. To be processed at all, data has to be decrypted and loaded into memory, which means encryption at rest and in transit no longer apply the moment it reaches this point. Historically, this meant anyone with access to the running system, including cloud provider staff or a compromised operating system, could potentially read that data.</p>

    <p>A <strong>secure enclave</strong> solves this by creating an isolated, hardware-protected region inside the processor. Data decrypted inside the enclave stays inside it. Even the operating system and the hypervisor running underneath cannot see what happens within that boundary. This class of hardware is generally called a <strong>TEE</strong>, a trusted execution environment.</p>

    <p>Think of it as a private room inside a shared office building where not even the building's own staff hold a key.</p>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Real-World Use Cases</h4>
        <p>Confidential computing platforms use secure enclaves to let multiple organizations process combined data sets without any party seeing the other's raw data. This is common in healthcare research and multi-party financial analysis, where the analytics job itself must never see unencrypted records outside the enclave.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- SVG: Secure enclave diagram
         Teaching purpose: show the learner that enclaves isolate data from the
         host OS and hypervisor itself, which they would otherwise misunderstand
         as just another form of ordinary encryption. -->
    <div style="margin: 20px 0;">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 760 150" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Secure enclave isolated within a processor">
            <rect x="40" y="15" width="680" height="120" rx="10" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="380" y="35" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-secondary)">Host System (OS and Hypervisor)</text>

            <rect x="280" y="50" width="200" height="65" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-primary)" stroke-width="2"/>
            <text x="380" y="75" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="var(--text-primary)">Secure Enclave</text>
            <text x="380" y="95" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="var(--text-secondary)">Decrypted data processed here</text>
            <text x="380" y="108" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="var(--text-secondary)">Hidden from host and hypervisor</text>
        </svg>
    </div>
</div>
HTML;

// TASK 6: Data Classification-Driven Policy Design
$task_contents[6] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Turning Controls Into Policy</h2>

    <p>Knowing individual controls is only half the job. A real encryption policy starts with classifying data by sensitivity, then requiring the correct controls for each tier across all three states. Skipping this step leads to two common failures: over-encrypting low value data and wasting resources, or under-encrypting sensitive data and creating real risk.</p>

    <p>Most organizations use a small number of tiers, commonly Public, Internal, Confidential, and Restricted. Think of it like a building's badge system: a Public tier needs no special access, while Restricted data requires several layers of locks working together before anyone can reach it.</p>

    <h3>Minimum Controls by Classification Tier</h3>
    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Tier</th>
                <th>In Transit</th>
                <th>At Rest</th>
                <th>In Use</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Public</td>
                <td>Optional</td>
                <td>Optional</td>
                <td>Not required</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Internal</td>
                <td>TLS or IPSec</td>
                <td>Disk encryption</td>
                <td>Not required</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Confidential</td>
                <td>TLS</td>
                <td>TDE</td>
                <td>Not required</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Restricted</td>
                <td>TLS or IPSec</td>
                <td>TDE with field-level encryption</td>
                <td>Secure enclave / TEE</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Balance Is the Goal</h4>
        <p>Applying Restricted-tier controls to Public data adds cost and complexity with no real benefit. Applying Public-tier controls to Restricted data leaves a genuine breach and compliance risk. A good policy maps effort to actual sensitivity, nothing more and nothing less.</p>
    </div>
</div>
HTML;

