$task_contents[8] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <p>Data protection is not a single control applied once. It is three separate problems, one for each state data can be in, each solved with a different set of tools. TLS and IPSec secure data while it moves, disk encryption, TDE, and field-level encryption secure it while it is stored, and secure enclaves protect it while it is actually being used.</p>

    <p>A classification-driven policy ties all of this together, making sure the level of protection applied actually matches how sensitive the data really is. These are the same decisions security architects make every day when designing real systems.</p>

    <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Protection requirements change depending on whether data is in transit, at rest, or in use.</li>
        <li>TLS protects individual connections; IPSec protects entire tunnels between networks.</li>
        <li>Disk encryption, TDE, and field-level encryption stack together rather than compete.</li>
        <li>Secure enclaves and TEEs protect data even from the host system it runs on.</li>
        <li>Classification tiers determine the minimum controls required in every state.</li>
    </ul>
</div>
